LEAD = {
    "headline": "LEVI'S SPENT FOUR HOURS OF FILMING ON EVERY SECOND OF ITS CHRISTMAS ADVERT, E.L.F. BUILT ITS FIRST EVER HOLIDAY CAMPAIGN BY HAND, AND THE BIGGEST MATCHED TEST ANYONE HAS PUBLISHED SAYS CREATIVE MADE BY A MACHINE PERFORMS THE SAME",
    "deck": "Three brands put visibly handmade work on the air inside six days.  Levi's shot a claymation yeti at four hours of filming per finished second.  E.l.f. made its first ever holiday campaign entirely in stop motion and counted the women who built it.  Prada hired a Disney animator to draw by hand.  Not one of them says the word AI.  And the one large test that has run machine-made and human-made creative against each other, same advertiser, same campaign, same day, found no difference once the matched pairs are controlled for.  So the craft is not being bought for performance.  Work out what it is being bought for before you price it.",
    "stamps": [
        ("LEVI STRAUSS & CO · 2 NOV", "https://www.levistrauss.com/2026/11/02/levis-holiday-2026/"),
        ("ADWEEK · 2 NOV", "https://www.adweek.com/brand-marketing/levis-rings-in-sweater-season-with-help-from-gus-a-cute-claymation-yeti/"),
        ("B&T · 3 NOV", "https://www.bandt.com.au/levis-gets-crafty-with-handmade-claymation-christmas-ad/"),
        ("E.L.F. RELEASE · 1 NOV", "https://www.stocktitan.net/news/ELF/e-l-f-made-to-stand-out-e-l-f-creates-handcrafted-holiday-campaign-wwpumwcayi96.html"),
        ("COSMETICS BUSINESS · 2 NOV", "https://cosmeticsbusiness.com/e-l-f-brands-opts-for-handcrafted-stop-motion-christmas-campaign"),
        ("CARTOON BREW · PRADA · 28 OCT", "https://www.cartoonbrew.com/advertising/john-pomeroy-prada-campaign-2d-handdrawn-268551.html"),
        ("PPC LAND · MATCHED AD TEST", "https://ppc.land/ai-ads-perform-as-well-as-humans-if-they-dont-look-ai-made/"),
        ("MARKETING WEEK · SYSTEM1", "https://www.marketingweek.com/dismiss-ai-ads-winning-creative-effectiveness/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "Start with Levi's, because Levi's published the number.  On Monday it launched Let's Get Fuzzy, a holiday campaign built around a claymation yeti called Gus, with knitwear rather than denim at the centre of it.  Levi's own release says the thing was created entirely with stop-motion claymation and that the sets, the characters and the outfits were fully crafted by hand.  Adweek supplies the number that matters: <mark>four hours of filming for every one second</mark> of finished animation.  Production and post went to Blinkink, directed by Stevie and Essy.  Global chief marketing officer Kenny Mitchell called it the celebration of the craft and human creativity synonymous with the Levi's brand.  The campaign nods back to Clayman, which Levi's ran in 1995.",
        "E.l.f. landed the same day with Made to Stand Out, which the company says is its first ever holiday campaign.  <mark>Sixty seconds</mark>, created entirely through handmade stop-motion animation, narrated by Alan Cumming, directed by Cami Kwan of Apartment D Films with concept and script from the agency SHADOW.  The brand's own count: <mark>women were 90 percent of the puppet team and 100 percent of the animation team</mark>.  It runs on e.l.f.'s digital and social channels plus takeovers of the television sections in Best Buy and Target.  Chief marketing officer Oshiya Savur put the positioning in one line: just as no two handcrafted creations are exactly alike, it is our differences that make each of us one of a kind.",
        "Prada got there five days earlier with Drawing Prada: Animated by Hand, live action mixed with two-dimensional hand-drawn winter landscapes and woodland animals, with Sadie Sink, Maya Hawke and Kelvin Harrison Jr in front of Glen Luchford's camera.  The animation director is <mark>John Pomeroy</mark>, a genuine Disney veteran, with Tom Bancroft as lead animator.  Prada put behind-the-scenes footage on its own site, which is the giveaway, and in it Pomeroy says there is a very distinct and purposeful reason to do hand-drawn animation, and that hand-drawn and handcrafted work is still alive in the world today.",
        "Now the part almost every write-up of this got wrong.  <mark>None of these brands mentions AI</mark>.  Mitchell talks about craft and human creativity and never says the word.  E.l.f.'s release never says it either, and the trade coverage that does confirms the brand's stated reason was its own consumer research on human creativity and nostalgia.  The anti-AI argument is being supplied by the press, not by the advertisers.  The one marketer on record making the link is Amanda Martinez of Ouai, who told Glossy that with the rise of AI people are craving really creative, really authentic content and do not want things they cannot trust.  She is also the source of the widely repeated claim that Prada went hand-drawn after a backlash to an AI campaign, and she flags that as online speculation.  Nobody else corroborates it.  The documented holiday AI backlashes belong to Coca-Cola and to McDonald's in the Netherlands.",
        "And then the evidence, which cuts the other way.  Researchers from Columbia Business School, Harvard, the Technical University of Munich and Carnegie Mellon ran a study announced on 28 January 2026 using what they call sibling adverts: machine-made and human-made creative from the same advertiser, in the same campaign, on the same day, served live on Taboola's Realize platform.  <mark>More than 500 million impressions and 3 million clicks</mark>.  The raw click-through rate was <mark>0.76 percent for AI creative against 0.65 percent for human-made</mark>, and once you control the matched pairs the two are <mark>statistically equivalent</mark>.  Separately, System1 tested 18 AI-assisted films against its database of 123,000 video adverts and the AI ones averaged <mark>3.4 of System1's five stars, its own predicted-growth score, against a 2.3 average</mark>, though seven of those 18 came from the agency group that co-authored the work.",
        "The finding inside the finding is the useful one.  <mark>Nearly half the AI adverts were taken for human-made</mark>, and the ones that did not read as AI outperformed both the human-made adverts and the ones that did read as AI.  So the penalty is not for being machine-made.  The penalty is for looking machine-made.  Which means four hours of filming per second is not buying you a better click rate, because the cheap version can get the same one.  It is buying you a thing that unmistakably could not have been generated, and an audience that has been told how long it took.",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("4 hours", "of filming Levi's spent on every single second of finished animation in its Christmas advert, according to Adweek"),
        ("0.76% v 0.65%", "raw click rates for AI-made and human-made adverts in a matched test across more than 500 million impressions, statistically equivalent once the pairs are controlled"),
        ("100%", "of the animation team on e.l.f.'s first ever holiday campaign were women, on the brand's own count"),
    ],
    "flagnote": "Levi's runtime is not published anywhere, and the three minutes on the Levi Strauss site is that page's reading time, not the film's length.  No creative agency is credited for Let's Get Fuzzy in any source that could be reached.  The four-hours-per-second figure is Adweek's, in a piece paywalled after four paragraphs.  E.l.f.'s team composition figures are the brand's own and its animation studio is not named.  Prada's runtime, number of films and production studio are not published, and Cartoon Brew credits Ferdinando Verderi as creative director while Variety credits Miuccia Prada and Raf Simons.  The matched advert study was announced in January 2026 and reported in February, so it is context rather than news; its platform is a content-recommendation network rather than YouTube, and click-through rate is not the same thing as brand effect.  The System1 work is not independent: seven of the 18 adverts came from Jellyfish and other Brandtech Group companies, and System1's own researcher co-authored it.  Nobody has published a head-to-head test of handmade craft against machine-made creative specifically, which is the narrower question this item is actually about.  The Canva and Harris Poll figure that 78 percent of consumers would rather see adverts made by people is stated preference from a company selling design tools, and is not performance data.",
    "so_what": "Strip the romance out and this is a pricing question.  If machine-made creative gets the same click rate, then the four hours per second are not buying attention, they are buying evidence of effort, and evidence only works on people who are shown it.  That is why all three of these campaigns come with behind-the-scenes footage, a studio credit and a number attached: the making-of is not a nice extra, it is the actual media.  Which also tells you how the whole thing fails.  Commission handmade work, release it with no account of how it was made, and you have paid a craft premium for a signal nobody received.  The audience cannot see four hours per second.  They can only be told.",
    "do_this": "On your next piece of commissioned creative, write the proof of effort into the brief as a deliverable, not as a favour.  Name the thing you will be able to say out loud afterwards: the number of frames, the hours per second, the people in the room, the week the puppets were built.  Then budget a second edit for it and give it a release date of its own, because that cut is what carries the premium you paid.  And when somebody argues craft on effectiveness grounds, go and read the matched test first, because the number is not on your side and you will lose that meeting.  Argue it on distinctiveness and on what the audience is being told, which is where it is actually true.",
}

